{"retractions" [[17592186327814 :question/answer-fields 17592186327815] [17592186327814 :question/answer-fields 17592186327817] [17592186327814 :question/problem "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}"] [17592186327814 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}=\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot 3+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A=1$ and $B=-2.$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186327816 17592186327818] "immutable_fields" [[17592186327815 :answer-field/id] [17592186327815 :answer-field/key] [17592186327815 :answer-field/type] [17592186327815 :answer-field/correct] [17592186327815 :answer-field/choices] [17592186327817 :answer-field/id] [17592186327817 :answer-field/key] [17592186327817 :answer-field/type] [17592186327817 :answer-field/correct] [17592186327817 :answer-field/choices]]}
